## What happened

Apple removed DSA from its cryptographic frameworks (`Security.framework` / `CryptoKit`). In .NET 11, the managed shim that previously called into those native APIs now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS when you call `DSA.Create()`. Linux (OpenSSL) and Windows (CNG) still support DSA, so those platforms are unaffected.

## How to fix it

**Option A — Use `DSAOpenSsl` explicitly on macOS** (requires OpenSSL via Homebrew):

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;

DSA GetDsa()
{
    if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    {
        // brew install openssl
        // Requires the OpenSSL engine; throws if OpenSSL isn't present.
        return new DSAOpenSsl();
    }
    return DSA.Create();
}
```

**Option B — Use a managed library (no native dependency):**

Add the `BouncyCastle.Cryptography` NuGet package and verify signatures with its pure-managed DSA implementation. This works identically on all platforms.

**Option C (recommended long-term) — Migrate away from DSA.**

DSA is considered legacy/weak. If you control both sides, re-sign with **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**, which are supported everywhere in .NET 11. If you only *verify* existing signatures, use Option A or B as a bridge while you sunset the old keys.

### TL;DR

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| `DSAOpenSsl` | Minimal code change | Requires OpenSSL on macOS |
| BouncyCastle | Fully cross-platform, no native dep | Extra NuGet dependency |
| Migrate to ECDSA/Ed25519 | Modern & secure | Requires re-signing |
